# One-Command Install And Run With Online Lookup

Use this guide if you want to download, install, and run Media-Prep-N-Sort from PowerShell with online metadata lookup enabled.

Online lookup helps the script suggest better folder names, categories, and genres by using public metadata sources. It uses Wikidata for movies, TVmaze for TV shows, MusicBrainz for music, and Open Library for books. No API key is required.

## Step 1: Open PowerShell

Open the Windows Start Menu, search for `PowerShell`, and open Windows PowerShell.

## Step 2: Copy And Paste The One-Line Command

Copy this entire command, paste it into PowerShell, and press Enter:

```powershell
$ProgressPreference='SilentlyContinue'; [Net.ServicePointManager]::SecurityProtocol=[Net.SecurityProtocolType]::Tls12; $choice=Read-Host 'Where do you want to install Media-Prep-N-Sort? Press Enter for Desktop'; if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($choice)) { $choice=[Environment]::GetFolderPath('Desktop') }; $choice=$choice.Trim().Trim('"'); $d=Join-Path $choice 'Media-Prep-N-Sort'; Write-Host ""; Write-Host "Install folder: $d"; $ok=Read-Host 'Are you sure? Type Y to download and run Media-Prep-N-Sort here'; if ($ok -in @('Y','y','YES','yes')) { New-Item -ItemType Directory -Force -Path $d | Out-Null; $base='https://raw.githubusercontent.com/hd9fxt7plq24vm6kcjxq2rw58y3z-cmyk/Media-Prep-N-Sort/main'; Invoke-WebRequest -UseBasicParsing -Uri "$base/MediaPrepNSort.ps1" -OutFile (Join-Path $d 'MediaPrepNSort.ps1'); Invoke-WebRequest -UseBasicParsing -Uri "$base/RUN_MEDIA_PREP_N_SORT.txt" -OutFile (Join-Path $d 'RUN_MEDIA_PREP_N_SORT.txt'); powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File (Join-Path $d 'MediaPrepNSort.ps1') -OnlineLookup } else { Write-Host 'Install cancelled. Nothing was downloaded.' }
```

## Step 3: Choose Where To Install Media-Prep-N-Sort

PowerShell will ask:

```text
Where do you want to install Media-Prep-N-Sort? Press Enter for Desktop
```

If you press Enter without typing anything, Media-Prep-N-Sort installs to your Desktop.

If you want to install it somewhere else, paste a folder path and press Enter.

Example:

```text
D:\Tools
```

The installer creates a folder named:

```text
Media-Prep-N-Sort
```

Everything downloaded by the command goes inside that folder.

## Step 4: Confirm The Install Location

PowerShell will show the final install folder and ask:

```text
Are you sure? Type Y to download and run Media-Prep-N-Sort here
```

Type:

```text
Y
```

Then press Enter.

If you type anything else, the install is cancelled and nothing is downloaded.

## Step 5: Enter Your Media Folder

After the script starts, it will ask:

```text
Where is your media folder?
```

To copy your media folder path, open your media folder in File Explorer, click the address bar at the top, press `Ctrl+C`, go back to PowerShell, press `Ctrl+V` or right-click to paste, and then press Enter.

Example folder path format:

```text
D:\My Media Folder
```

## Step 6: Review The Script Suggestions

The script scans only the top-level folders inside your media folder.

It can suggest a category, a cleaner folder name, a genre for music or books, an online metadata match, and a confidence level.

The script can sort into:

```text
1 = MOVIES
2 = TV SHOWS
3 = MUSIC
4 = BOOKS
```

If the script is unsure, it asks you what to do. You can choose a category, accept a suggestion, type a corrected name, skip a folder, or cancel.

## Step 7: Final Safety Confirmation

Before anything moves, the script shows a summary.

Nothing changes unless you type both confirmations exactly.

First type:

```text
MOVE
```

Then type:

```text
CONFIRM
```

After `CONFIRM`, folder moves and renames begin. There is no built-in undo after that point.

If you type anything else at either confirmation, the script stops safely and nothing changes.

## Cancel Anytime Before Moving

Before the final move starts, you can cancel at normal prompts by typing:

```text
Q
CANCEL
EXIT
STOP
```

The script will quit cleanly.

## What Gets Downloaded

The one-line command downloads these files into the `Media-Prep-N-Sort` folder:

```text
MediaPrepNSort.ps1
RUN_MEDIA_PREP_N_SORT.txt
```

Reports are not created by default.

## How To Run It Again Later With Online Lookup

If you used the default Desktop install location, you can run it again from any PowerShell window with:

```powershell
powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File "$([Environment]::GetFolderPath('Desktop'))\Media-Prep-N-Sort\MediaPrepNSort.ps1" -OnlineLookup
```

If you installed it somewhere else, use this universal finder command:

```powershell
$roots=@([Environment]::GetFolderPath('Desktop'),(Join-Path $env:USERPROFILE 'Downloads'),(Get-Location).Path)|Where-Object{$_ -and (Test-Path -LiteralPath $_)}; $s=Get-ChildItem -LiteralPath $roots -Filter 'MediaPrepNSort.ps1' -Recurse -Depth 3 -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue|Select-Object -First 1 -ExpandProperty FullName; if(-not $s){$f=Read-Host 'Where is your Media-Prep-N-Sort folder?'; $s=Join-Path $f.Trim().Trim('"') 'MediaPrepNSort.ps1'}; if(Test-Path -LiteralPath $s){powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File $s -OnlineLookup}else{Write-Host 'Could not find MediaPrepNSort.ps1.'}
```

The shorter command below only works if PowerShell is already open inside the `Media-Prep-N-Sort` folder:

```powershell
powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File ".\MediaPrepNSort.ps1" -OnlineLookup
```

## How To Run It Again Later And Save Reports

Open PowerShell inside the `Media-Prep-N-Sort` folder and run:

```powershell
powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File ".\MediaPrepNSort.ps1" -OnlineLookup -SaveReports
```

## How To Preview Without Moving Anything

Open PowerShell inside the `Media-Prep-N-Sort` folder and run:

```powershell
powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File ".\MediaPrepNSort.ps1" -OnlineLookup -DryRun
```

## How To Remove Media-Prep-N-Sort

Delete the `Media-Prep-N-Sort` folder.

That removes the script, helper text file, and any optional reports saved inside that folder.

It does not delete your media.
